 (TOTAL OF LINES 805 TO 885)","form":"RS","row":"890","col":"1"}},{"label":"Net revenue expenditure","code":"RS_netrevexp_net_exp","by_year":{"2017-18":15040.0,"2018-19":15824.0,"2019-20":16698.0,"2020-21":18253.0,"2021-22":18596.0,"2022-23":18961.0,"2023-24":18952.0,"2024-25":24396.0},"prov":{"description":"NET current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":144147,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":5597000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":35784,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":39503,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2575.74,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":375.34,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Preston council has 18 months to set its last budget before local authority merger","lead":"Preston City Council will cease to exist on 1 April 2028, when a new unitary authority takes over running services in the area. The Cabinet has confirmed that the 2027/28 budget will very likely be the council's final financial plan. The council faces a statutory deadline of 10 March 2027 to set council tax, the annual local levy residents and businesses pay to fund local services. The timetable paper warns explicitly that missing this deadline would put the council \"in breach of the statutory process\" and create \"a financial impact on the Council\". Councillors have already built contingency dates into their schedule\u20145 March and 8\u201310 March\u2014should the primary meeting need deferral. Once parliament approves the Structural Changes Order formally establishing the reorganisation, new spending and asset-transfer controls will come into force, though the council has yet to publish the detail of what those restrictions will mean.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Cabinet Budget timetable 27_28","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62335/62335.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Strategy 2026-27 Mid Year Review September 2026 with appendix","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62417/62417.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation process has been paused, removing planned transitional funding calls but creating medium-term uncertainty about council structure.","detail":"The council had set aside \u00a31m in earmarked reserves for LGR costs with plans to pay Lancashire County Council \u00a3839,876 in quarterly instalments over two years. Following the pause of the LGR programme, this process is now under review. This removes a planned near-term funding call but creates uncertainty about eventual reorganisation costs and the council's future structure.","category":"governance","matters":"Lenders need to understand major structural changes. The pause improves near-term cash position but eventual reorganisation could have significant implications for the council's standalone financial future.","figures":"\u00a31m set aside in earmarked reserves; planned payments \u00a3839,876 over two years, now on hold","quote":"This process is currently under review as a result of the pause of Local Government Reorganisation.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Strategy 2026-27 Mid Year Review September 2026 with appendix","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62417/62417.pdf","headline":"Investment income exceeding forecast by \u00a31.029m in 2026/27 due to higher bank rates and larger cash holdings, supporting deferral of external borrowing.","detail":"The council has benefited from the Bank of England maintaining bank rate at elevated levels. In-house investment income is forecast to increase by \u00a3660k for 2026/27, and external fund managers are delivering an additional \u00a3369k for 2026/27 and \u00a3183k for 2027/28. This additional \u00a31.029m in 2026/27 treasury investment returns is contributing to the council's ability to defer external borrowing and maintain strong cash positions.","category":"income","matters":"Strong treasury management delivering above-forecast returns provides a cash buffer, reduces reliance on external borrowing in the near term, and demonstrates competent financial management.","figures":"In-house investment income increase \u00a3660k; external fund managers increase \u00a3369k (2026/27); total treasury improvement 2026/27 \u00a31.029m","quote":"Increase in investment income returns due interest rates set by the Bank of England remaining high and due to holding higher cash balances than forecast","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Strategy 2026-27 Mid Year Review September 2026 with appendix","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62417/62417.pdf","headline":"Business rates income growing with \u00a31.9m additional growth in 2025/26 now flowing into 2026/27 forecast, and Collection Fund showing \u00a34.394m accumulated surplus.","detail":"The council's business rates income is strengthening: 2025/26 outturn showed \u00a31.9m growth, now included in the 2026/27 forecast. The Collection Fund for NNDR has an accumulated surplus of \u00a34.394m as at 31 March 2026, of which the council's share is \u00a31.758m. However, the council has written off a number of business rates debts due to business closures, indicating some collection risk in a post-pandemic economy.","category":"income","matters":"Business rates growth improves the council's revenue base and forecast resilience. Collection fund surpluses provide a buffer, but write-offs suggest collection challenges remain.","figures":"Business rates growth 2025/26 \u00a31.9m; Collection Fund NNDR surplus \u00a34.394m; council's share \u00a31.758m","quote":"In 2025/26 outturn, the Council reported business rates growth of \u00a31.9m which has been included in this forecast","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Financial Strategy 2026-27 Mid Year Review September 2026 with appendix","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62417/62417.pdf","headline":"Council has no underlying structural deficit and forecasts return to surplus from 2028/29, with general fund revenue surplus expected in those years.","detail":"The mid-year update shows the council in balance for 2026/27 and 2027/28 (with planned reserve support for growth investments), then forecasts surpluses of \u00a31.108m in 2028/29 and \u00a3516k in 2029/30. Crucially, these are not weather-dependent variances but reflect structural improvement in the forecast position compared to March 2026.","category":"savings","matters":"The absence of structural deficit demonstrates the council's core revenues are sufficient to meet ongoing commitments without requiring emergency savings or service cuts.","figures":"2026/27 balanced with \u00a3953k planned reserve call; 2028/29 surplus \u00a31.108m; 2029/30 surplus \u00a3516k; 2029/30 call on reserves reverses to contribution of \u00a3516k","quote":"general fund revenue surplus is expected in 2028/29 and 2029/30.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"- FINAL Appendix A - Audit Committee Annual Report 2025-2026","paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62390/62390.pdf","headline":"The council is facing reorganisation challenges while delivering major projects.","detail":"The Audit Committee, in its forward look for 2026/27, notes the importance of effective governance given the council's reorganisation challenges and major project delivery. The specific nature and financial impact of these challenges is not detailed in this report.","category":"governance","matters":"Council reorganisations typically involve operational and financial changes; unexplained challenges warrant monitoring by lenders assessing risk.","figures":"None","quote":"In looking forward to 2026/27 and beyond, given the reorganisation challenges facing the Council and the on-going delivery of major projects, the importance of an effective Audit Committee remains critical.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Preston","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":false,"headline":"Statutory deadline for Council Tax is 10 March 2027; missing it creates financial and legal jeopardy.","lead":"Missing statutory deadlines creates financial loss and legal breach; the council's tight schedule depends on precept authorities (county, fire, police) meeting their own deadlines by 28 February.","also":["1 other finding in this window"],"watch":null,"n_findings":2,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Cabinet Budget timetable 27_28","top_paper_url":"https://preston.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62335/62335.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":62,"news_drafts_total":1}}